\details{
\subsection{LOCF}{

For landmark \eqn{s}, subject \eqn{i} and variable \eqn{v}, the value is
the last \emph{non-missing} measurement of \eqn{v} at time \eqn{t \le s} (or
\eqn{t < s} when \code{include_at_landmark = FALSE}), provided it is within
\code{lookback}. Each variable is handled separately, so a visit where one
variable is missing still contributes the others. Selection depends only
on \code{id} and \code{time}, never on row order. Any column type works, and the type
(including factor levels) is preserved. The time since each
carried-forward measurement is stored in the metadata for auditing.

If a subject has two \emph{different} non-missing values of an \code{locf} variable
at the same time, the function errors and lists the affected subjects and
times rather than choosing one arbitrarily. Exact duplicates are collapsed.
}

\subsection{LME}{

For every \code{lme} variable \eqn{v} and landmark \eqn{s}, an independent
Gaussian model is fitted with \code{\link[lme4:lmer]{lme4::lmer()}} to the non-missing
measurements, taken at or before \eqn{s}, of the subjects in the risk set at
\eqn{s}:

\deqn{Y_{ij} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 (t_{ij} - s) + \gamma^\top x_i + b_{0i} [+ b_{1i}(t_{ij} - s)] + \epsilon_{ij}}

where \eqn{x_i} are the optional \code{adjust} terms. Time is centred at the
landmark and rescaled internally for numerical stability, which does not
change the predictions. The derived value is the conditional prediction at
\eqn{t = s} including the subject's random effects
(\code{predict(fit, re.form = NULL)}), i.e. \eqn{\hat\beta_0 + \hat\gamma^\top
x_i + \hat b_{0i}}. A subject with a single measurement is kept; their
prediction is shrunk towards the population trajectory. A subject with no
measurement before the landmark, or with missing \code{adjust} values, gets
\code{NA} rather than a population-only prediction. Different values recorded
at the same time are treated as replicate measurements.

Each fit gets a status: \code{"ok"}; \code{"warning"} when \code{\link[lme4:lmer]{lme4::lmer()}} warned
(e.g. about convergence) or the fit is singular (\code{\link[lme4:isSingular]{lme4::isSingular()}}); or
\code{"failed"} when there were too few subjects or \code{\link[lme4:lmer]{lme4::lmer()}} gave an
error. Failed fits give \code{NA} for that variable at that landmark without
affecting other fits, and a warning lists every fit that was not \code{"ok"}.
There is no automatic fallback from a random slope to a random intercept.

Standard errors in a second-stage survival model treat LME-derived values
as known, so they understate uncertainty.
